.\" Man page for axkill
.\"
.TH AXKILL 8 "06 Oct 2026" Linux "Linux System Managers Manual"
.SH NAME
axkill \- terminate a running AX.25 connection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B axkill
[\-v]
.I port dest src
.br
.B axkill
[\-v]
.IR port : dest
.I src
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B axkill
aborts an established AX.25 connection without touching the program that owns
it.  The connection is the one from
.I src
to
.I dest
on
.IR port ,
and all three have to be right: the kernel has no way to find the connection
itself, and a request that names another one simply finds nothing.
.PP
The request travels down to the program through the same channel
.BR axctl (8)
uses, and on a port served by
.BR ax25netd (8)
it arrives as an AGWPE
.B Q
control frame, so the session is torn down there and a radio link is
disconnected at the upstream rather than only in this program's opinion.
That is the point of the tool: a mailbox session that has stopped answering
can be cleared without restarting the program that opened it.
.PP
Nothing is reported when it works.  There is no output on success, because
there is no question the caller did not already know the answer to \(em and a
line of output on success would be read as a warning by whatever runs this
from a script.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I port
The port, as it is written in
.BR axports (5):
an
.BR ax25netd (8)
upstream name, optionally with a channel behind a colon
.RB ( radio0:2 ),
a
.B wampes
node or
.BR wampes: port ,
or a kernel AX.25 interface name.
.TP
.I dest
The destination callsign of the connection.
.TP
.I src
The source callsign of the connection.
.TP
.B \-v
Print the version and exit.
.PP
.I port
and
.I dest
may be written as one argument,
.IR port : dest .
The split is at the
.I last
colon, not the first: a callsign contains no colon, and the one colon a port
name may carry is the channel of an axports entry, so
.B radio0:2:DB0AAA
is the port
.B radio0:2
and the destination
.BR DB0AAA .
Cutting at the first colon would leave the
.B 2
in the destination, which is not a callsign and would be refused \(em and
.B radio0:2
would look like a port with a destination in it that nobody meant.
.PP
The channel of a port is therefore addressed through the
.BR axports (5)
entry and not on this command line:
.B radio0:2
has to be a line in that file.  There is no
.B /2
form here.  That spelling is
.BR ax25tcpd (8)'s,
and it exists because there the channel is chosen when the connect is typed
rather than configured once; here the connection is looked up by the three
addresses it already has, and a fourth piece of syntax to say which of
sixteen channels it is on would be one more thing to get wrong in a command
whose whole job is to find something that is hard to see.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the request was made.  This says the request was delivered to the
stack, not that a connection was there to be killed: the ioctl succeeds
whether or not anything matched, so a wrong callsign is not reported and
leaves no trace.  1 when the port name is not one
.BR axports (5)
knows, or the stack refuses the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B axkill radio0 DB0XYZ DL9SAU-4
Kill the link from DL9SAU-4 to DB0XYZ that
.B call
made over
.BR radio0 .
.TP
.B axkill radio0:2:DB0XYZ DL9SAU-5
The same on channel 2, written as one argument.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
